course: english
lesson: 58
title: Olympiad: Breakthrough puzzles in made-up languages
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In a UKLO Breakthrough puzzle (UKLO is the UK Linguistics Olympiad, and Breakthrough is its first, easiest level), you meet a language you have never seen and work out its rules from a few examples, like a detective.
explain: You never need to know the language beforehand: everything is in the data, and the trick is to line the examples up and spot what changes and what stays the same.
explain: Look for repeated bits: if kapa is house and kapakapa is houses, then saying a word twice makes a plural, so sela (tree) gives selasela (trees).
explain: Word order may not work like English: in some languages a little ending, not the place in the sentence, shows who is doing the action.
explain: Always check your rule against EVERY example before you answer, and if the data does not tell you something, the honest answer is "the data does not say".
fact: In Tolo, saying a noun twice makes it plural: kapa (house), kapakapa (houses), miru (dog), mirumiru (dogs).
fact: In Ruvan, mika is girl, pela is dog, tor is sees and sun is feeds; "Mika-n pela tor" means the girl sees the dog.
fact: In Ruvan, "Pela-n mika tor" and "Mika pela-n tor" both mean the dog sees the girl, so the ending -n marks the one doing the action, wherever it stands.
fact: In Kel, pa = 1, ki = 2, ro = 3, mu = 4 and lan = 5; a number after lan is added (lan pa = 6), and a number before lan multiplies it (ki lan = 10, ro lan = 15), so ki lan pa = 11.
fact: In Nam, ka- at the front means it happened in the past, and -ti at the end means "you": nalu = I swim, ka-nalu = I swam, nalu-ti = you swim, ka-nalu-ti = you swam.
fact: In Selk, the describing word comes first: ki (eye) + ota (water) = kiota (tears, "eye-water"); with fen (fire) and mor (mountain), a fire-mountain, a volcano, is fenmor.
fact: In Lumi, -sa makes a plural, -i means "my" and -ou means "your", added in that order: tak (cat), taki (my cat), takou (your cat), taksa (cats), taksai (my cats).
fact: In Pirr, bo (big) and lo (small) go in front of the noun: bopir = big bird, lopir = small bird, bomuk = big fish.
fact: In Tano, ne goes just before the verb to mean "not": Ti ama = I like, Ti ne ama = I don't like, Ka ama = you like.
fact: In Ilo, -ko turns a doing word into the person who does it: dami (to run), damiko (runner), seri (to sing), seriko (singer).
fact: In Vor, ral is red, sel is blue and ralsel is purple, but no example shows selral, so the data does not say what it means.
exercise: Tolo: kapa = house, kapakapa = houses. If sela = tree, how do you say "trees"? | selasela | selas | kapasela | selaselasela
exercise: What is the Tolo rule for making plurals? | Say the word twice | Add -s to the end | Put kapa in front | Say it 3 times
exercise: Ruvan: "Mika-n pela tor" = The girl sees the dog. Which word means girl? | mika | pela | tor | the -n
exercise: Ruvan: "Mika pela-n tor" = The dog sees the girl. What does the ending -n show? | Who is doing the action | Who is being seen | That there are two | That it is in the past
exercise: Ruvan: "Mika-n pela sun" = The girl feeds the dog. What does "Pela mika-n sun" mean? | The girl feeds the dog. | The dog is feeding the girl. | The girl watches the dog. | The dog watches the girl.
exercise: Kel: what number is "lan pa"? | 6 | 5 | 10 | 51
exercise: Kel: what number is "ki lan"? | 10 | 7 | 25 | 12
exercise: Kel: what number is "mu lan ro"? | 23 | 12 | 19 | 27
exercise: Kel: how do you say 14? | ki lan mu | mu lan ki | lan mu | ki mu lan
exercise: Nam: ka-nalu = I swam, nalu-ti = you swim, vemu = I eat. How do you say "you ate"? | ka-vemu-ti | vemu-ti | ka-vemu | ti-vemu-ka
exercise: Selk: ki = eye, ota = water, kiota = tears, fen = fire, mor = mountain. Which word means volcano? | fenmor | morfen | fenota | kimor
exercise: Lumi: taksa = cats, taki = my cat, takou = your cat, taksai = my cats. How do you say "your cats"? | taksaou | takousa | taksa | takoui
exercise: Pirr: bopir = big bird, lopir = small bird, bomuk = big fish. How do you say "small fish"? | lomuk | bomuklo | lobomuk | lopirmuk
exercise: Tano: Ti ama = I like, Ti ne ama = I don't like, Ka ama = You like. How do you say "You don't like"? | Ka ne ama | Ne ka ama | Ka ama ne | Ti ne ka
exercise: Ilo: dami = to run, damiko = runner. If tasa = to teach, what is "teacher"? | tasako | kotasa | tasami | damitasa
exercise: Vor: ral = red, sel = blue, ralsel = purple. What does "selral" mean? | The data does not say | Purple, for certain | Green, for certain | It is not a word
test: Your mate says, "You can't do UKLO unless you already speak loads of languages." Put him right. | Everything you need is in the data | You must already speak it | You must speak three languages | Only grown-ups may take part
test: Kai, from Liverpool: "Tolo's dead easy, our kid, 'dogs' is mirus." ("Dead" means very.) What is right? | mirumiru | mirus | mirumirumiru | kapamiru
test: A Ruvan cartoon says the first word always does the seeing, like in English. Fix it. | The -n ending shows who sees, not the order | The cartoon is right; the first word sees | The last word in the sentence always sees | Nobody ever sees anybody in Ruvan
test: Kel: what number is "ro lan pa"? | 16 | 9 | 31 | 15
test: Nam: what does "ka-nalu" mean? | I swam | You swam | I will swim | You swim
test: Lumi: you have "taki" (my cat) and want "my cats". What do you say? | taksai | takisai | takiksai | taksaki
test: Selk: which word would mean "mountain water", water from a mountain? | morota | otamor | fenota | kiota
test: Gran from the Black Country: "It's bostin' how yow cracked it, bab." ("Bostin'" means great; "bab" is a fond word for a child.) Tano: how do you say "I don't like"? | Ti ne ama | Ti ama ne | Ne ti ama | Ka ne ama
test: Ilo: what does "seriko" mean? | Singer | To sing | A runner | A little song
test: Mia's rule for Pirr is "lo means big". Which example proves her wrong? | lopir = small bird | bopir = big bird | bomuk = big fish | She is right
test: Kel: Josh says 20 is "lan mu". Check against the data. How do you really say 20? | mu lan | lan mu | ki lan ki | ro lan mu
exam: Identify the principle stated to govern UKLO puzzles concerning prior knowledge of the language. | All that is required is contained in the data | Fluency in the language is presupposed | A dictionary of the language is permitted | A knowledge of Latin is essential
exam: It is given that, in the language Tolo, "kapa" denotes "house" and "kapakapa" denotes "houses". Determine the plural of "sela", denoting "tree". | selasela | selas | kasela | sela
exam: Ascertain the value of the Kel numeral "mu lan ro", given that a numeral preceding "lan" multiplies it and one following "lan" is added to it. | Twenty-three | Twelve | Seventeen | Twenty-seven
exam: Select the response consistent with the data concerning the meaning of the Vor word "selral". | The data do not establish its meaning | It denotes purple beyond all doubt | It denotes the colour green | It denotes the absence of colour
